    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "reconnecting feels impossible though. how can i find \"strength\" in something that's just broken? it's not even about being sad, it's just... it's like the story reached its end. the athlete is gone. the person who actually lived was the one who could move, compete, feel alive. what's left is just the credits rolling on a movie that's already finished. does it even make sense to keep watching when the main character is dead?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's completely understandable why you feel this way when your core identity was tied to physical capability. However, the story isn't necessarily over; it might be shifting to a new genre. Strength doesn't only reside in performance; it can be found in resilience, mindfulness, and simply existing through discomfort. Allowing yourself to grieve the loss of the athlete is important, but also explore what parts of \"you\" remain that are independent of the physical.",
      "source": "target",
